<h2 id="s-method" data-toc="How this was made">How this was made</h2>
<p>The site lists every livestream in each channel's streams tab and stream playlists, unlisted VODs included. For each one it downloads the chat replay and reads out every superchat, super sticker and gifted membership. Some streams have no chat data. YouTube keeps no chat replay for some of them, usually because the VOD was edited after the stream or chat was off, and others are members-only or age-restricted.</p>
<p>The site matches people by YouTube channel ID, so someone who renamed their account still counts once. A cross-channel donor is anyone who paid on two or more of the channels at any point. The filters change which of their superchats count, not who counts as cross-channel. Superchats in other currencies are converted to US dollars at the exchange rates of <span id="fxdate"></span>, not the rate on the day they were sent.</p>
<p>Gifted memberships have no price in the chat. A gift always buys the channel's cheapest membership level, so the site values each one at that level's US price, which is <span id="prices"></span>. Those values are estimates, so they stay out of the dollar totals unless you tick "Count gifted memberships". Gifts carry no message, so a message filter leaves them out. Memberships a channel gifts from its own account don't count, and regular memberships aren't counted at all.</p>
<p>The amounts are what viewers paid, and YouTube keeps about 30% of that. The site reads only public chat. Dates are the day the stream started.</p>
<p>Download the full data as CSV: <a href={`${base}data/cross-channel-donors.csv`}>cross-channel donors</a>, <a href={`${base}data/donors.csv`}>all donors</a>, <a href={`${base}data/superchats.csv`}>every superchat</a>, <a href={`${base}data/gifts.csv`}>gifted memberships</a>, <a href={`${base}data/streams.csv`}>streams</a>. These files ignore the filters.</p>
